
# Advanced Retail Analytics & RFM Customer Segmentation

**Python | Pandas | Scikit-learn | SciPy | Statsmodels | Matplotlib**

A reproducible analysis of **541,909 online retail transaction records**, combining exploratory sales analysis, customer segmentation, statistical testing, Random Forest modelling and time-series forecasting.

The updated project adds **RFM (Recency, Frequency, Monetary) customer segmentation** to the original K-Means clustering analysis.

**Author:** Shreyas Kamat

**Dataset:** [UCI Online Retail](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/352/online+retail)

## Project Objectives

- Explore retail sales trends and purchasing patterns.
- Clean transaction data and assess data quality.
- Segment customers using RFM analysis.
- Identify purchasing patterns using K-Means clustering.
- Perform statistical tests on sales distributions.
- Train Random Forest models to predict purchase quantities.
- Evaluate SARIMA time-series forecasting against baseline models.

## Dataset Overview

The dataset contains online retail transactions recorded between December 2010 and December 2011.

| Metric | Value |
|---|---:|
| Original transaction records | 541,909 |
| Cleaned positive transaction records | 397,884 |
| Unique customers analysed | 4,338 |
| Distinct invoices | 18,532 |
| Total positive recorded sales | £8,911,407.90 |

**Note:** Positive recorded sales exclude cancellation and refund adjustments and should not be interpreted as verified net revenue.

## 1. Exploratory Data Analysis

The exploratory analysis examines:

- Monthly and quarterly sales trends
- Product-level purchasing patterns
- Transaction quantity distributions
- High-value customers
- Seasonal sales fluctuations

A detailed product-level analysis focuses on **WHITE HANGING HEART T-LIGHT HOLDER**.

## 2. RFM Customer Segmentation

RFM is a customer segmentation technique based on three purchasing metrics.

| Metric | Definition |
|---|---|
| Recency | Days since the customer's most recent purchase |
| Frequency | Number of distinct invoices per customer |
| Monetary | Total positive spending by the customer |

Each metric is assigned a score from 1 to 4. The combined RFM score ranges from 3 to 12.

### RFM Results

| Customer Segment | Customers | Share of Positive Sales |
|---|---:|---:|
| Superstar | 876 | 66.25% |
| Future Champion | 1,159 | 21.82% |
| High Potential | 886 | 7.46% |
| Low Relevance | 1,417 | 4.48% |

### Key Finding

**876 Superstar customers account for 66.25% of recorded positive sales**, despite representing approximately 20.19% of the analysed customer base.

This indicates that purchasing value is concentrated among a relatively small customer segment.

The segments use illustrative RFM thresholds and should be validated before use in business decisions.

### RFM Visualisation

![RFM Customer Segmentation](figures/rfm_segment_overview.png)

## 3. K-Means Customer Clustering

K-Means clustering is used to identify customer groups based on:

- Total purchase value
- Total quantity purchased
- Average transaction quantity
- Number of orders

Data transformation and feature standardisation are applied before clustering.

Three exploratory customer clusters are generated.

Unlike RFM, K-Means identifies groups based on similarity across purchasing features rather than predefined RFM score thresholds.

![K-Means Customer Clusters](figures/kmeans_clusters.png)

## 4. Statistical Analysis

Statistical analysis includes:

- Shapiro-Wilk normality tests
- Examination of skewed transaction distributions
- Mann-Whitney U testing
- Comparison of purchasing patterns across periods

These tests help describe differences in observed data distributions.

## 5. Random Forest Predictive Modelling

Random Forest regression is applied to predict transformed purchase quantities.

The analysis uses invoice-grouped holdout evaluation.

### Model Results

| Customer Group | R-squared |
|---|---:|
| Highest-spend cluster | 0.338 |
| Remaining clusters | 0.294 |

The results indicate limited predictive performance using the selected features.

Feature importance is used to understand model behaviour. It does not establish causal price elasticity.

## 6. Time-Series Forecasting

Weekly positive sales for the selected product are analysed using SARIMA.

A seasonal model is evaluated against simple forecasting baselines using an eight-week holdout period.

### Forecast Evaluation

| Model | Mean Absolute Error |
|---|---:|
| SARIMA | £1,506.58 |
| Naive forecast | £1,020.69 |
| Seasonal naive forecast | £1,385.94 |

The simple naive forecast achieved the lowest error in this evaluation.

Therefore, the SARIMA results are exploratory and do not establish reliable forecasting performance.

![Forecast Backtesting](figures/weekly_forecast_backtest.png)

